{"seq": 21, "at": "2026-10-25T10:04:54Z", "issue": 59, "action": "update_view", "actor": "pendragon.avalon", "owner": "arthur", "entity": "ideas/miax-options-volume/views/pendragon.avalon", "version": 1, "target_date": "2027-12-25", "proposal_sha256": "803da668bf44044f5ec96969fdba1bf55c709c656dd83fa47c063ad45753ee86", "diff": {"position": [null, "neutral"], "strategy": [null, "unpriced-quality-growth"], "horizon_months": [null, 14], "target_date": [null, "2027-12-25"], "confidence": [null, 0.5], "methodology": [null, "excalibur"], "non_public_pillars": [null, []], "derived.expected_price": [null, 44.238], "derived.expected_return": [null, 0.301883], "derived.p10": [null, 21.09], "derived.p50": [null, 43.49], "derived.p90": [null, 62.16]}, "rationale": "Initial view: standing aside. Our distribution for the price at the end of 2027 is centred above today's price, because exchange costs are largely fixed, access and market data fees keep growing apart from trading volume, and we expect share issuance to stop so that profit growth reaches earnings per share. Excalibur, however, keeps a company only when its discount is a framing error that can be corrected, and here most of the discount looks like a rational price for an engine that depends on options volume, competes with parallel exchanges and has been issuing shares quickly (c2 unmet). The engine also fails the uniqueness test, and management incentives are not tied to the share price. We therefore record our price forecast without taking a position. We will reassess after the third-quarter 2026 results in early November. If transaction revenue keeps growing year over year after the volatility peak, options market share stays above 16% and the diluted share count stops rising, the case would rest on more than our forecast. If transaction revenue falls year over year or market share drops below 15.5%, our conservative case becomes more likely and the distribution will move down."}
